19:T5d4,## Pass it along

An expense report needs approval. A Manager can sign off up to $1000, a Director up to $10000, the CEO for anything above. The employee shouldn't have to know who has authority, they just submit, and the request flows up until someone with the right limit handles it.

## The pattern

**Chain of Responsibility** decouples the **sender** of a request from its **receiver**. Each handler holds a reference to the **next** handler and decides, at runtime, whether to *handle* the request or *pass* it along:

```
abstract class Approver {
    Approver next;
    void setNext(Approver a){ next = a; }
    void handle(Request r){
        if (canApprove(r)) approve(r);
        else if (next != null) next.handle(r);   // forward
    }
}
```

You **build the chain at runtime**, `manager.setNext(director); director.setNext(ceo)`so the order, length, and membership are all configurable without touching any handler.

## Real uses

- **Approval workflows**, expenses, leave requests, escalations.
- **Middleware pipelines**, auth → logging → rate-limit → route (Express, servlet filters).
- **Logging levels**, a logger forwards to the next sink if it can't handle the severity.
- **Event bubbling**, a DOM event climbs the parent chain until something handles it.

## Trade-off

A request can **fall off the end unhandled**, if no handler in the chain accepts it, it silently does nothing. Always provide a default/terminal handler or make "unhandled" an explicit outcome.c:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-h-screen","children":[["$","$L17",null,{"activeModule":"lld-behavioral","activeLesson":"chain-of-responsibility"}],["$","$L18","chain-of-responsibility",{"lesson":{"id":"chain-of-responsibility","patternId":"lld-behavioral","title":"Chain of Responsibility","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"Core","statement":"Pass a request along a chain of handlers; each either handles it or forwards it to the next, so the sender doesn't need to know who will deal with it.","companies":["Amazon","Google","Atlassian"],"theory":"$19","approaches":[{"label":"The chain","code":["abstract class Approver { Approver next; void setNext(Approver a){...} void handle(Request r){...} }","class Manager extends Approver { ... }    // limit $1000","class Director extends Approver { ... }   // limit $10000","class CEO extends Approver { ... }        // unlimited"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"The abstract Approver holds a `next` handler, the self-pointing diamond IS the chain link, and defines handle() to either approve or forward, plus setNext() to wire the chain.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"h","name":"Approver","stereotype":"abstract","fields":["- next: Approver"],"methods":["+ handle(req)","+ setNext(a)"],"x":1,"y":0.2,"tint":"accent"},{"id":"mgr","name":"Manager","methods":["+ handle()"],"x":0,"y":1.6},{"id":"dir","name":"Director","methods":["+ handle()"],"x":1,"y":1.6},{"id":"ceo","name":"CEO","methods":["+ handle()"],"x":2,"y":1.6}],"relations":[{"id":"m","from":"mgr","to":"h","kind":"inherit"},{"id":"d","from":"dir","to":"h","kind":"inherit"},{"id":"c","from":"ceo","to":"h","kind":"inherit"},{"id":"next","from":"h","to":"h","kind":"aggregate","label":"next"}],"shownClassIds":["h"],"shownRelIds":["next"],"activeIds":["h"]},"vars":[["holds","next: Approver"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":2,"narration":"Manager, Director, and CEO are concrete handlers. Each extends Approver and carries its own approval limit, overriding handle() with the rule it knows.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["h","mgr","dir","ceo"],"shownRelIds":["next","m","d","c"],"activeIds":["mgr","dir","ceo"]}},{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"At runtime you link them: Manager → Director → CEO. Each one will either approve the request or pass it upward to its next, the sender just talks to the head of the chain.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["h","mgr","dir","ceo"],"shownRelIds":["next","m","d","c"],"activeIds":["h"]},"vars":[["chain","Manager → Director → CEO"]]}]},{"label":"Pass it up the chain","code":["employee.submit(expense);   // goes to the Manager","manager.handle(req);        // over my limit → next.handle(req)","director.handle(req);       // within my limit → approve()"],"steps":[{"view":"sequence","codeLine":1,"narration":"An Employee submits a $5000 expense to the Manager, the only handler they know about.","sequence":{"actors":[{"id":"e","label":"Employee","kind":"actor"},{"id":"m","label":"Manager"},{"id":"d","label":"Director"}],"messages":[{"id":"m1","from":"e","to":"m","label":"approve($5000)","kind":"call"},{"id":"m2","from":"m","to":"d","label":"approve($5000)","kind":"call"},{"id":"m3","from":"d","to":"d","label":"within my limit ✓","kind":"self"},{"id":"m4","from":"d","to":"e","label":"approved","kind":"return"}],"shownCount":1,"activeIndex":0}},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":2,"narration":"The Manager's limit is $1000, this is over budget, so it FORWARDS the request to the next handler, the Director. The employee never knows this hop happened.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:messages","shownCount":2,"activeIndex":1},"vars":[["Manager","limit $1000 → pass"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":3,"narration":"The Director's limit covers $5000, so it handles the request itself instead of passing it on.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:messages","shownCount":3,"activeIndex":2},"vars":[["Director","limit $10000 → approve ✓"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":3,"narration":"The Director returns approved straight back to the Employee. The request flowed down the chain until someone could deal with it, and the sender only ever talked to the Manager.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:messages","shownCount":4,"activeIndex":3},"vars":[["Director","limit $10000 → approve ✓"]]}]}]}}]]}]
